{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEvaluating a Pass-1 change on Sonnet when production runs Opus (or vice versa) confounds the measurement: prior work showed Sonnet degrades past ~3 criteria per turn while Opus is flat, so a delta could be the model, not the change. Two further hazards: the production model classes (`rebar.toml` `[llm.model_classes]`: frontier = `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, standard = `bedrock:…claude-sonnet-4-6`) carry an Anthropic-direct fallback, and `_intersect_capabilities` (`src/rebar/llm/runner_support.py`) collapses a mixed chain's `prompt_cache_style` to `none` — which is why cache reads dropped to zero on 2026-08-12 and review cost roughly doubled. An eval run that silently fell back to Anthropic would both break the \"Bedrock only\" requirement and lose caching.\n\n## Approach\n\nBuild `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/parity.py` as a standalone module in THIS ticket (`tier1.py`, `tier2.py`, and `report.py` do not exist yet — they are built by later tickets in this epic, `presolar-finable-binturong`/Tier-1, `peaceable-choppy-sapsucker`/Tier-2, and the report module bundled with the Tier-0 ticket `bouncy-peacockish-titmouse`; THIS ticket only builds and unit-tests `parity.py`'s functions in isolation, exposing the API those later tickets will import and call — it does not itself edit tier1.py/tier2.py/report.py, which are out of scope here):\n\n- **Pinned targets**: `resolve_pinned_model(pass_name)` maps `pass_name` to a production model class via a fixed constant `PASS_MODEL_CLASS = {\"pass1\": \"frontier\", \"pass2\": \"standard\"}` (Pass 1 = finders = frontier/Opus, Pass 2 = verifier = standard/Sonnet, matching the epic's own tier definitions; Pass 3/decide is deterministic and never resolves a model, so it is not in this map — calling with an unmapped `pass_name` raises `ValueError`). It reuses the EXISTING model-class resolution seam rather than re-parsing `rebar.toml` by hand: `rebar.llm.model_classes.load_class_slots()` for the resolved slots, `resolve_class(name, slots)` for the class's primary `provider:model` string (raises `LLMConfigError` for an unknown class — not reimplemented here), and requires that primary string to start with `bedrock:`, refusing (raising) otherwise. `resolve_fallback_chain(name, slots)` is called only to confirm there IS a fallback configured (documented context, not used to select a candidate) — the homogeneous-chain guarantee comes from refusing a non-Bedrock PRIMARY, not from picking among fallbacks; this ticket's `resolve_pinned_model` never returns a fallback candidate. `check_model_parity(pass_name, resolved_id, baseline_ran_model)` refuses when `resolved_id` differs from a supplied baseline `ran_model` for that pass, unless an explicit `allow_model_change=True` is passed, in which case the result is labelled `model-change` and callers must never diff it against a baseline with a different id. (The later Tier-1/Tier-2 tickets are responsible for sourcing `baseline_ran_model` from a corpus row's `provider_provenance.ran_model` and for wiring `--allow-model-change` as a CLI flag — this ticket's function takes it as a plain argument.)\n- **Result stamping helper**: `stamp_pass_model(result, pass_name, model_id)` and `refuse_diff_on_model_mismatch(result_a, result_b)` — the latter raises when two result dicts carry different per-pass model ids under a `models: {pass: model_id}` key. (Later tickets call this from `report.py`, which does not exist yet.)\n- **Caching check**: `check_cache_effective(usage_rows)` asserts from a list of usage-log row dicts that `cache_read_tokens > 0` on at least one row after the first, returning a `{\"cached\": bool, \"first_cache_hit_row\": int | None}` verdict callers can fold into a report or the ledger. (Later tickets call this after a live run and fold the verdict into the report/ledger — those integrations don't exist yet.)\n- No production config is changed by this ticket; the homogeneous chain is composed in-process, in this module only.\n\n**Corpus field addition needed by this ticket's own tests and by later parity checks**: `corpus.py`'s `_build_sidecar_row` (`src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/corpus.py`) currently emits a row with `verdict`/`material_fingerprint`/etc. but does not surface `provider_provenance.ran_model` from the sidecar's `data` dict. Add a `ran_model` key to the emitted row (`data.get(\"provider_provenance\", {}).get(\"ran_model\")`, `None` when absent). This is additive to the row SHAPE (no existing key removed or renamed) but IS a material content change: `_compute_manifest` hashes every key of every row (`content_hash = sha256(json.dumps(rows, sort_keys=True))`), so adding `ran_model` changes `content_hash` for every future corpus build — the already-committed `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/replay/corpus-2026-08-29.manifest.json` becomes stale and must be regenerated and re-committed as part of this ticket's own integration step (same procedure celestine's own ticket used), not silently left mismatched.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/parity.py` (new)\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/corpus.py` (edit: add `ran_model` to the sidecar row)\n- `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/replay/corpus-<date>.manifest.json` (regenerated: new content_hash from the added row field)\n- `tests/unit/evals/test_plan_replay_parity.py` (new)\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit: `resolve_pinned_model(\"pass1\")` and `(\"pass2\")` resolve the frontier/standard Bedrock ids (via `load_class_slots`/`resolve_class`) respectively; an unmapped pass_name (e.g. `\"pass3\"`) raises `ValueError`; a config whose frontier class resolves to a non-Bedrock primary is refused (patch `load_class_slots`/`resolve_class` to return a fixture Anthropic id); `refuse_diff_on_model_mismatch` raises on differing per-pass model ids and accepts identical ones; `check_cache_effective` marks a usage-log fixture with zero cache reads as uncached and one with `cache_read_tokens > 0` on a later row as cached. A corpus row built from a sidecar carrying `provider_provenance.ran_model` surfaces it as `ran_model`; one without the key surfaces `ran_model: None`. Live check (cache effectiveness against a real run) happens as part of the Tier-1 reproduction run in a later ticket, not here.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `resolve_pinned_model` maps `pass1`/`pass2` to the frontier/standard Bedrock ids via the existing `model_classes.load_class_slots`/`resolve_class` seam and refuses a resolved primary that is not a Bedrock id; checked by unit tests.\n- [ ] `refuse_diff_on_model_mismatch` raises when two result dicts carry differing per-pass model ids and accepts identical ones; checked by a unit test.\n- [ ] `check_cache_effective` correctly classifies a cached vs. an uncached usage-row fixture; checked by a unit test.\n- [ ] `corpus.py`'s sidecar row surfaces `ran_model` from `provider_provenance.ran_model`, absent-safe; the corpus manifest is regenerated so its `content_hash` reflects the new field; checked by a unit test and by re-running the corpus build.\n- [ ] Unit tests pass in `make test`.\n- [ ] No LLM calls; checked by running with no provider credentials."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788051788794716259,"uuid":"2526f690-1bcc-45c6-9efb-6b97bb50fbca"}